From Residual Questions to Specified Failures: Individually Calibrated Persuasion under EU Digital Law

A Theoretical Thesis

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

1. The Problem and the Shape of the Argument

This thesis begins from a deflationary observation. The claim that EU digital law suffers a general "benchmark collapse" in the face of individually calibrated persuasion is too strong to survive contact with the instruments. The AI Act regulates manipulative techniques; the proposed Digital Fairness Act addresses dark patterns, unfair personalisation and addictive design; the GDPR governs profiling; the DSA governs platform risk. Individual targeting is not an unregulated category. It is a category regulated by several instruments whose operative provisions are anchored in population-based benchmarks, event-level harm, and standards of knowledge that probabilistic inference does not easily satisfy.

The thesis therefore narrows the object. What remains unresolved is not whether the law recognises individual targeting, but whether three interlocking operational difficulties prevent it from seeing a particular class of conduct: the selection of a persuasive technique because of an inferred individual susceptibility, sustained across sessions, in an ordinary commercial context. These difficulties are labelled qualification (can the conduct be characterised under a statutory category?), evidence (can the profiling logic, interaction history and optimisation records be obtained?), and attribution (can it be shown that the technique was selected because of the inferred susceptibility rather than because it is generally effective?).

The methodological commitment of the thesis is that these difficulties must be converted from residual questions into falsifiable claims. A residual question can be gestured at indefinitely; a falsifiable claim can be rejected. The contribution is therefore not a new right against influence. It is a specified failure typology and a two-path agenda for testing which failure, if any, occurs for which variant of conduct. The thesis is theoretical: it reports no original empirical data, and it advances conditional claims whose truth depends on legislative text not yet settled and on empirical work not yet executed.

2. The Instruments and the Redundancy Test

The legal architecture must be stated precisely, because the gap hypothesis fails or succeeds at the level of individual provisions rather than at the level of "the law" as a whole.

AI Act Article 5(1)(a) prohibits AI systems deploying subliminal, purposefully manipulative or deceptive techniques that materially distort behaviour by appreciably impairing the ability to make an informed decision, causing significant harm. Its structure is event-specific and threshold-dependent. Article 5(1)(b) prohibits exploiting vulnerabilities due to age, disability, or a specific social or economic situation, with the same material-distortion and significant-harm requirements. The Commission's guidelines on prohibited practices give the financial-stress scenario a concrete home: they contemplate predictive targeting of predatory financial products at persons in dire financial situations, and they indicate that transient circumstances such as temporary unemployment or over-indebtedness may qualify, while excluding diffuse conditions that are not socio-economically specific.

The proposed DFA (COM(2026) 1100) supplies the consumer-protection layer. Article 10 prohibits interface designs likely to materially distort economic behaviour and blacklists enumerated dark patterns. Articles 12 and 13 address unfair personalisation: Article 12 restricts profiling-based advertising where the trader knows with reasonable certainty that the individual is a minor; Article 13 restricts personalised pricing where it is reasonably likely to harm minors or vulnerable consumers. Article 17 requires traders to provide accessible tools to disable enumerated addictive features. The general vulnerability provision is expected to build on UCPD Article 5(3) and to extend it to "any specific misfortune or circumstance of such gravity as to impair the consumer's judgement."

Two consequences follow immediately, and both cut against the original gap hypothesis.

First, the redundancy test must begin with Article 5(1)(b), not with the DFA. If the financial-stress variant falls within the AI Act's "specific social or economic situation" limb—and the Commission's own example suggests it may—then the gap hypothesis must be rejected for that variant unless a residual failure remains. Second, the DFA's personalisation provisions are structurally child- and pricing-centric. Article 12 protects minors; Article 13 addresses pricing harms. Adult-directed, strategy-level persuasive personalisation—where the presentation and strategy are calibrated to inferred psychological state without altering the price—falls between these provisions and the contested general clause. This is the defensible version of the gap claim: not that the DFA fails to regulate personalisation, but that its specific personalisation provisions are under-inclusive for adults, leaving the conduct to a general clause whose interpretation is uncertain.

3. Path A: The Legal Mapping and Its Honest Limits

The financial-stress variant is the tractable test case: a conversational shopping assistant infers from interaction data that a user is in financial difficulty and uses that inference to select and calibrate the promotion of a high-interest credit product. Three layers of analysis follow.

The technique layer. If the assistant deploys false urgency or false scarcity, the technique is independently unlawful under Article 10 irrespective of personalisation, and the three-difficulty framework is not engaged. The legally interesting case is the technique that is not independently unlawful—a true statement that an offer is time-limited—but is calibrated to an inferred vulnerability. At this layer Article 10 does not apply, and the analysis must proceed to the vulnerability and personalisation provisions.

The vulnerability layer. The falsifiable claim is that inferred financial stress can be characterised as a "circumstance of such gravity as to impair judgement" within the broadened vulnerable-consumer standard, such that the practice is assessed from the perspective of the average member of the group of financially stressed consumers. This claim is testable against the final text and against interpretive guidance. It is, however, a claim of characterisation, and characterisation claims are weak unless the decision criteria are stated in advance. The thesis therefore specifies them: the claim is confirmed if the final text retains the "circumstance of such gravity" formulation and guidance treats inferred financial stress as capable of constituting such a circumstance; it is rejected if the text narrows the vulnerability concept or treats the inference as insufficiently grave. The same discipline applies to the interaction between the general clause and Articles 12–13: the claim that the general fairness or aggressive-practices framework reaches conduct the specific provisions do not is confirmed only if the final text does not treat the specific provisions as exhaustive of personalisation.

The certainty layer. The DFA's "reasonable certainty" standard is a distinct and more tractable limitation than the general vulnerability claim. A probabilistic inference of financial stress from interaction data may not meet a standard of reasonable certainty, and the standard creates a perverse incentive: a trader who deliberately avoids knowing an inference can escape Article 12 or 13. The thesis treats this as a testable textual limitation and asks whether the DFA's evidence-shifting powers could compel disclosure of probabilistic inference thresholds and confidence scores.

The addictive-design layer. The original claim that relationship-simulation in a financial-stress context constitutes an "addictive feature" within Article 17 is almost certain to fail. Article 17 requires tools to disable enumerated compulsive-use features; it does not ban them, and a one-off credit pitch is not within the enumerated categories absent a delegated act. The thesis therefore drops this claim and retains Article 17 only as a contingent route if a delegated act expands the list.

Two further caveats belong to the mapping rather than to a footnote. The DFA is a proposal; the Parliament and Council may narrow or broaden the vulnerability concept, the certainty standards, or the evidence-shifting powers, and any mapping is therefore time-sensitive rather than final. And the mapping must check whether sectoral financial-services or consumer-credit rules displace the DFA for the credit variant—a question this thesis could not resolve and which it therefore flags as a scope condition rather than assumes away.

4. Path B: The Research Protocol and Its Construct-Validity Problem

The legal mapping tests whether the DFA's categories can see the conduct. The research protocol tests whether the conduct produces a measurable loss of decision autonomy beyond ordinary personalisation. The two are linked: if the conduct produces no such loss, the case for a distinct legal category weakens, because the harm the category is supposed to address is not empirically demonstrable.

The protocol retains a three-condition structure—standard recommendation, declared-preference personalisation, and inferred-susceptibility targeting—but its design must be stated with the limits the sources impose.

The Zeng et al. architecture is a feasibility demonstration, not an operational model of the targeted conduct. The system conditions a strategy-level policy on an 81-dimensional personality embedding predicted turn by turn from generated utterances, and its composite reward incorporates agreement intent, donation amount and change-of-mind penalties. It demonstrates that turn-level psychological conditioning is technically tractable. It does not estimate urgency susceptibility or reassurance need, and its domain is charitable donation with simulated dialogues. The thesis therefore cites it for tractability only, and specifies the additional instrumentation required to operationalise the legally relevant susceptibility constructs.

The Rauchfleisch and Jungherr baseline is cross-study, and the protocol must not assume it transfers. The confirmed figures are a 12.6-point control shift, 13.1 points with AI-identity disclosure (practically equivalent), and 6.3 points with persuasive-intent disclosure—roughly a halving—in 1,500 UK adults on policy attitudes with a chatbot identical for all participants. The protocol's central comparison is whether a calibrated variant produces a larger residual effect after intent disclosure than a uniform variant. If it does, that is evidence that the calibrated variant operates through a mechanism transparency does not reach; if the residual is comparable, the case for a distinct category weakens. But a factorial design (calibrated versus uniform, crossed with disclosure) requires substantially more power than three conditions, and the thesis pre-registers a power analysis rather than borrowing the baseline's precision.

The outcome measures require reconstruction. Decision confidence and perceived pressure are proxies for "appreciable impairment," not operationalisations of it. Post-cooling-off revision is confounded by the existing withdrawal right. A stronger test of the "decision the person would not otherwise have made" element elicits pre-interaction preferences and compares them with post-interaction choices. Persistence and escalation across sessions operationalise the cumulative-harm dimension that event-based rules cannot capture, and the protocol should include a cumulative condition testing whether the aggregated loss crosses the significant-harm threshold even when no single session does.

The ethical compromise has an inferential cost that must be stated in the design. Operationalising financial stress through a validated scenario-based prime rather than actual financial data is defensible; but it tests the activation of a stress mindset, not the effect on people who are actually financially distressed. This is a construct-validity boundary condition, not a flaw to be argued away, and it limits external validity accordingly.

The design has a further limit that honesty requires stating plainly. It measures behaviour in a controlled setting. It cannot establish that a real trader deployed the calibrated variant with intent to exploit. It can establish only that the calibrated variant produces a measurable autonomy effect that ordinary personalisation does not. That suffices for the harm dimension; it does not resolve attribution.

5. Attribution: The Reward Function, Not the Selection Log

The attribution difficulty is the hardest and is not resolved by either path alone. Logs showing that strategy selection depends on inferred traits do not distinguish exploitation from benign personalisation, because nearly all personalisation does this. The informative evidence is the reward function—what the system was optimised to maximise—and the guardrails that constrained the policy. A trader optimising for conversion without a welfare constraint is differently situated from one whose reward includes change-of-mind penalties or user-welfare metrics, and the Zeng et al. composite reward illustrates the difference in principle.

Two consequences follow. First, the legal question is not whether attribution is technically tractable but whether the DFA's evidence-shifting powers are drafted broadly enough to compel preservation and disclosure of reward-function specifications and guardrail designs, not merely strategy-selection logs. Second, prompt-based LLM assistants may leave no strategy-level logs at all, which strengthens rather than weakens the evidence-difficulty claim: the very architecture that makes calibrated persuasion cheap also makes its reconstruction expensive. The thesis therefore proposes a disclosure duty targeting reward-function specification and guardrail design, and acknowledges that such a duty does not yet exist in the text under examination.

6. The Specified Failure Typology

The thesis's positive contribution is a typology that replaces the general gap hypothesis with three specific failures, each with a decision criterion.

A qualification failure occurs where the conduct falls outside every statutory category—where Article 5(1)(b) does not reach inferred transient stress, the DFA's specific provisions do not reach adult-directed strategy personalisation, and the general clause is interpreted not to bridge the difference. A evidence failure occurs where the categories apply in principle but the records needed to establish the inference, the selection and the optimisation objective cannot be obtained or interpreted, including the limiting case of prompt-based systems that leave no strategy-level logs. An attribution failure occurs where the records exist but do not distinguish selection-because-of-inferred-susceptibility from general effectiveness, and where no duty to disclose the reward function closes the gap.

The financial-stress variant is the test case for the typology, not an illustration of a predetermined conclusion. If Article 5(1)(b) covers the variant, the gap hypothesis is rejected for it and the typology records a qualification success. If it does not, the failure must be specified as qualification, evidence or attribution, and the policy response calibrated to that failure—legislative amendment for qualification, evidentiary powers for evidence, disclosure duties for attribution. This is the sense in which the thesis is conditional: it does not assert a gap; it specifies what would count as one.

7. The Limits of the Argument, Stated Plainly

Four limits must be stated rather than buried.

First, the legislative object is unsettled. The DFA is a proposal; the Parliament has not adopted its position. Every Path A claim is a claim about a text that may change, and the mapping is therefore time-sensitive and provisional rather than final.

Second, some sources could not be independently verified. The precise status of a Parliament briefing on digital vulnerability, and the standing of "dynamic vulnerability" as anything more than a contested scholarly and policy proposal, remain uncertain. The thesis cites these as normative and interpretive resources, not as established legislative intent, and it does not treat the digital-vulnerability vocabulary as codified law. Industry objections that dynamic vulnerability is operationally unworkable are recorded as part of the debate rather than dismissed.

Third, the empirical protocol is a proposal, not a result. No data are reported. The protocol's construct validity is unresolved, its power analysis is pre-registered rather than executed, and its ethical compromise limits its external validity. The thesis claims only that the protocol is pre-registrable and that its outcomes map onto the legal elements non-trivially.

Fourth, the thesis is theoretical and normative in part. The claim that the DFA's personalisation provisions are under-inclusive for adults is a claim about legislative design, not a claim that a court would so hold. The claim that a disclosure duty should target reward functions is a normative proposal, not a description of existing law. Distinguishing these registers is part of the argument's honesty.

8. Conclusion

The thesis advances a narrower and more defensible claim than the benchmark-collapse thesis it replaces. The unresolved problem is not the recognition of individual targeting but three operational difficulties—qualification, evidence and attribution—whose presence must be demonstrated variant by variant rather than asserted globally. The financial-stress variant is the tractable test case, and the AI Act's Article 5(1)(b), read with the Commission's guidelines, is the instrument most likely to settle it; the DFA's specific personalisation provisions are the residual site of a possible adult-directed gap, and the "reasonable certainty" standard is a more testable limitation than the contested general vulnerability clause. The research protocol is pre-registrable but must be reconstructed around direct preference elicitation, a factorial power analysis, and an explicit construct-validity boundary. Attribution is best pursued through reward-function and guardrail disclosure rather than selection logs, and the absence of strategy-level logging in prompt-based systems strengthens rather than weakens the evidence-difficulty claim.

The object of the research is not to establish a new right against influence. It is to determine whether the existing instruments can see what they are looking at, and to specify with precision what they cannot see. The contribution is a typology of specified failures and a two-path agenda for testing them—offered, as it must be, conditionally.

APPENDICES:

Appendix A: Legal Provisions Mapping — The Digital Fairness Act (COM(2026) 1100)

This appendix records the operative textual anchors in the proposed DFA, distinguishing confirmed provisions from design features that remain contingent on the final legislative text.

A.1 Institutional and Regulatory Form. The DFA was formally presented by the Commission on 11 November 2026 as COM(2026) 1100 final. It takes the form of a directly applicable Regulation, replacing the Unfair Commercial Practices Directive (UCPD) in its entirety and creating a single EU rulebook for business-to-consumer commercial practices, online and offline. The accompanying overhaul of the Consumer Protection Cooperation Regulation would grant the Commission direct enforcement powers, including fines of up to 6% of worldwide annual turnover for widespread infringements with a Union dimension.

A.2 Article 10 — Dark Patterns. Article 10 prohibits designing, organising or operating online interfaces in ways likely to deceive, manipulate, or otherwise materially distort consumers’ economic behaviour. Specific dark patterns are prohibited outright, with the precise contents of the blacklist subject to a delegated act and future expansion by the Commission.

A.3 Articles 12–13 — Unfair Personalisation. Article 12 restricts profiling-based advertising directed at consumers known with reasonable certainty to be minors. Article 13 restricts personalised pricing based on profiling where it is reasonably likely to cause harm to minors or vulnerable consumers. Crucially, the provision is framed around pricing and minors, not around adult-directed persuasive strategy selection.

A.4 Article 17 — Addictive Design. Article 17 requires traders to provide accessible and effective tools allowing consumers to disable addictive features individually or collectively. The enumerated features include autoplay, endless feeds without effective breaks, notifications unrelated to direct user activity, and features that encourage frequent engagement through penalties or loss of benefits. The Commission may update the list through delegated acts. The provision requires a disablement tool, not a prohibition of the feature itself.

A.5 The General Vulnerability Clause and the "Circumstance of Such Gravity" Formulation. The DFA is expected to build on UCPD Article 5(3), extending it to cover "any specific misfortune or circumstance of such gravity as to impair the consumer's judgement." The Ashurst analysis notes the structure of the instrument: many new prohibitions target only minors or vulnerable consumers, but outside those groups, the general fairness clause still applies. Whether inferred financial stress qualifies as a "circumstance of such gravity" remains a question of interpretation against the final text.

A.6 Evidence-Shifting Powers. The DFA proposes that courts and authorities could order companies to disclose how an online practice is designed and how it affects consumer behaviour, and could draw adverse inferences from a refusal. This is the textual basis for the evidence-difficulty analysis in the main text; its scope—whether it reaches reward-function specifications and guardrail designs—is not specified.

