Appendix D: The European Parliament's "Digital Vulnerability" and "Digital Asymmetry" Concepts

This appendix records the policy vocabulary that supplies the normative argument for closing the adult-directed personalisation gap, while noting its contested status.

D.1 The Parliament Briefing (10 November 2026). The European Parliament Think Tank briefing "From Average to Vulnerable Consumer in Digital Environments" recommends that the future DFA introduce the concepts of digital vulnerability and digital asymmetry to recognise their impact on consumer choices and behaviour. Digital asymmetry is defined as the structural imbalance in information, technical capability and insight into consumer behaviour between businesses and consumers.

D.2 The Analytical Framework. Scholarly elaboration treats digital asymmetry as the structural relationship between platform and consumer, and digital vulnerability as the condition of the consumer. A related formulation defines digital professional diligence as "not exploiting digital asymmetry and/or digital vulnerability by a trader towards consumers". Digital vulnerability is described as a universal state of susceptibility to the exploitation of power differences in the trader–consumer relationship that result from factors beyond the consumer's control.

D.3 The Industry Objection and the Status of "Dynamic Vulnerability." The concept of dynamic vulnerability—a situational or inferred condition rather than a stable characteristic—has been formally contested. A coalition of digital advertising bodies including IAB Europe has cautioned the Commission against relying on a broad definition, arguing that it creates legal uncertainty and incentivises additional personal-data collection. Industry analysis argues that expanding vulnerability into a dynamic or situational condition renders it operationally unworkable, because it cannot be objectively identified or proven in real time, and enforcement would require intrusive profiling. The thesis therefore cites digital vulnerability and digital asymmetry as normative and interpretive resources, not as codified law, and records the industry objection as part of the debate.

