Appendix E: UCPD Article 5(3) — The Baseline Vulnerability Standard

This appendix records the pre-DFA legal baseline, which the DFA proposes to broaden and which supplies the interpretive frame for the "circumstance of such gravity" formulation.

E.1 The Operative Text. UCPD Article 5(3) addresses commercial practices likely to materially distort the economic behaviour only of a clearly identifiable group of consumers who are particularly vulnerable to the practice or the underlying product because of their mental or physical infirmity, age or credulity. The practice is assessed from the perspective of the average member of that group.

E.2 The Commission's Guidance. Recital 19 provides a non-exhaustive list of characteristics that make a consumer "particularly susceptible," and the Commission's guidance records that vulnerability is multi-dimensional: a consumer may be vulnerable in one dimension and not another, and most consumers show signs of vulnerability in at least one dimension. The guidance defines the vulnerable consumer as one who, as a result of socio-demographic characteristics, behavioural characteristics, personal situation or market environment, is more susceptible to certain marketing practices—the dimension most relevant to Article 5(3).

E.3 The "Exceptional Character" Limit. The case law and commentary treat the higher level of protection under Article 5(3) as an exceptional case relative to the average-consumer standard. This is the doctrinal reason why the DFA's broadened formulation is significant: it potentially converts an exceptional protective standard into a more general fairness obligation, which is precisely the textual choice the Path A mapping must track.

