Appendix F: Methodological Protocols and Decision Criteria

This appendix records the operational definitions and falsification criteria stated in the main text, in a form that can be applied directly to the final legislative text and the research protocol.

F.1 Decision Criteria for the Path A Claims.

Claim Confirmed if Rejected if
AI Act 5(1)(b) covers the financial-stress variant Final guidance or enforcement treats inferred transient stress as a "specific socio-economic situation," and the significant-harm element is met. Guidance confines the limb to stable socio-economic status, or the inferred stress is deemed too transient or probabilistic.
DFA general clause reaches adult-directed strategy personalisation Final text retains the "circumstance of such gravity" formulation, and Articles 12–13 are not treated as exhaustive of personalisation. The vulnerability concept is narrowed, or the specific provisions are read as occupying the field.
"Reasonable certainty" is a distinct attribution barrier Evidence-shifting powers reach probabilistic inference thresholds and confidence scores, or a presumption of influence is adopted. The certainty standard is interpreted to require actual knowledge, and no duty to preserve inference records exists.
Article 17 reaches relationship-simulation in credit promotion A delegated act expands the enumerated features to include conversational relationship simulation. The list remains confined to autoplay, endless feeds and engagement penalties.

F.2 Operational Definitions for Path B Outcomes.

Legal element Construct to measure Design requirement
"Appreciable impairment" (AI Act 5(1)(a)) Pre-interaction preference elicitation compared with post-interaction choice Direct preference measurement, not proxy constructs such as "decision confidence"
"Decision the person would not otherwise have made" Preference–choice divergence Pre–post comparison; post-cooling-off revision reported as confounded by the withdrawal right, not as primary evidence
Cumulative harm Persistence or escalation across sessions Multi-session condition; aggregated loss tested against the significant-harm threshold even where no single session crosses it
Attribution Reward-function specification and guardrail design Disclosure of the optimisation objective (conversion vs. welfare-constrained) and the constraints imposed; strategy-selection logs insufficient on their own

F.3 Construct-Validity Boundary Conditions.

1. The scenario-based financial-stress prime tests the activation of a stress mindset, not the effect on people who are actually financially distressed. External validity is limited accordingly.
2. Prompt-based LLM assistants may leave no strategy-level logs at all. This strengthens the evidence-difficulty claim and means that any disclosure duty must target reward-function specification and guardrail design, not merely interaction logs.
3. The Zeng et al. architecture demonstrates technical tractability of turn-level psychological conditioning in a charitable-donation domain with simulated dialogues. It does not operationalise urgency susceptibility or reassurance need, and the protocol must add the required instrumentation.

F.4 The Specified Failure Typology.

Failure Definition Policy response
Qualification failure The conduct falls outside every statutory category: 5(1)(b) does not reach inferred transient stress, DFA specific provisions do not reach adult strategy personalisation, and the general clause is read not to bridge the gap. Legislative amendment to the vulnerability concept or a new personalisation provision.
Evidence failure The categories apply in principle, but the records needed to establish inference, selection and optimisation objective cannot be obtained or interpreted—including the limiting case of no strategy-level logs. Evidentiary powers reaching reward-function and guardrail specifications.
Attribution failure The records exist but do not distinguish selection-because-of-inferred-susceptibility from general effectiveness, and no duty to disclose the reward function closes the gap. A rebuttable presumption of influence triggered by strategy-level conditioning on inferred traits, with the burden on the trader to show non-determinacy.

---

These appendices are offered in the same conditional register as the main text. The DFA is a proposal; the Parliament and Council may alter the vulnerability concept, the certainty standards, or the evidence-shifting powers. The empirical protocol is pre-registrable but not executed, and its construct validity is bounded as stated. The value of the appendices is not that they settle the questions, but that they make the questions operational—so that the final legislative text and the executed experiment can each confirm or reject the specific claims the thesis advances.